  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWe have some mixed cloudscapes out there this afternoon with\ndeparting mid and high level clouds over northwest Wisconsin and\nsome incoming stratocumulus along the International Border\nassociated with an upper-level trough. Some of those clouds will\npersist overnight, mainly across the Arrowhead, with clearing\nelsewhere. The challenge overnight is overnight lows and\nfiguring out just how cold it will get. We will have high\npressure moving in tonight, and most places should see clear\nskies. However, at least some pressure gradient will persist\nfurther east, keeping winds from becoming calm in parts of\nnorthwest Wisconsin and especially along the North Shore with\nsome downslope flow. With that said, most guidance highly favors\ntemperatures falling to around or below 36 degrees for inland\nareas tonight (70-90% chance) with lesser probabilities for\nfreezing or below temperatures (40-60% up north where Freeze\nWarnings are already in effect and 20-50% for parts of interior\nnorthwest Wisconsin). With all that in mind, I expanded Frost\nAdvisories to cover all zones except the North Shore, where\nbreezy conditions will probably prevent frost there even if\ntemperatures do get towards freezing for some places. Some\nplaces within a Frost Advisory may not see frost, like around\nthe Brainerd Lakes or near Lake Superior. In addition, some\nplaces in interior northwest Wisconsin could see freezing\ntemperatures, but confidence is not high enough that it will be\nwidespread enough to warrant Freeze Warnings. All that to say,\nit should be cold tonight, and there will probably be some\nlocality to frost/freeze conditions, but in general, most places\nwill probably see some frost. \n\nOn Friday, expect quiet and sunny weather with high pressure and\nseasonably cool temperatures. As high pressure moves east Friday\nnight, parts of the Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin will still\nbe under the influence of that with mostly clear skies and light\nwinds. Additional frost/freeze headlines will likely be needed.\nFurther west in north-central Minnesota, southerly winds will\npick up a bit and temperatures won't be quite as cold. Frost\nwon't be as likely as a result. Instead, expect increasing\nclouds with some showers moving in with an incoming shortwave\ntrough that's expected to pass along the Canadian border. Models\ncontinue to favor an area of mainly light rain developing and\ntargeting the International Border on Saturday with lingering\nshowers into Saturday evening. Rainfall amounts continue to look\nnot-so-impressive with most places picking up a tenth or two of\nan inch at best. \n\nA bit of rinse and repeat going into Sunday as a cold front\npasses through Saturday night and we end up with cool northwest\nflow on Sunday. High pressure settles in Sunday night, and we'll\nbe looking at another widespread frost/freeze. Coverage of\nfreezing temperatures will probably be much greater compared\nto tonight, though some areas (like near Lake Superior) may\nstill evade a hard freeze. \n\nSoutherly flow will likely bring warm and above normal\ntemperatures early next week starting on Monday. Rain chances\nare looking pretty sparse next week, but it's possible there\ncould be some sprinkles or light rain from time to time.",
